<worksheet xml:id="worksheet-lintrans-basic">
<title>Discovery guide</title>

<activity xml:id="activity-lintrans-basic-matrix">
	<introduction>
		<p>
		An <m>m \times n</m> real matrix <m>A</m> creates a function
		<m>\funcdef{T_A}{\bbR^n}{\bbR^m}</m> by matrix multiplication:
		<me> T_A(\uvec{x}) = A \uvec{x} </me>.
		We will call such a function a <term>matrix transformation</term> <m>\bbR^n \to \bbR^m</m>.
		</p>
	</introduction>
	<task><p>
		Write out linear input-output component formulas for function <m>T_A</m> associated to matrix
		<me> A = \begin{abmatrix}{rrr} 1 \amp 2 \amp -3 \\ 2 \amp -1 \amp 5 \end{abmatrix} </me>,
		so that <m>T_A(\uvec{x}) = \uvec{w}</m>:
		<me>
			\begin{sysofeqns}{rcrcrcr}
				w_1 \amp = \amp <fillin characters="2" /> x_1 \amp + \amp <fillin characters="2" /> x_2 \amp + \amp <fillin characters="2" /> x_3 \text{,} \\
				w_2 \amp = \amp <fillin characters="2" /> x_1 \amp + \amp <fillin characters="2" /> x_2 \amp + \amp <fillin characters="2" /> x_3 \text{.}
			\end{sysofeqns}
		</me>
	</p></task>
	<task xml:id="activity-lintrans-basic-matrix-from-formulas"><p>
		Determine the matrix <m>B</m> so that the linear input-ouput component formulas below correspond to a matrix transformation <m>\uvec{w} = T_B(\uvec{x})</m>.
		<me>
			\begin{sysofeqns}{rcrcr}
				w_1 \amp = \amp 3 x_1 \amp - \amp x_2 \\
				w_2 \amp = \amp 5 x_1 \amp + \amp 5 x_2 \\
				w_3 \amp = \amp  \amp + \amp 7 x_2 \\
				w_4 \amp = \amp - x_1 \amp + \amp x_2
			\end{sysofeqns}
		</me>
	</p></task>
	<task xml:id="activity-lintrans-basic-matrix-against-std-vecs"><p>
		Suppose you know that matrix transformation <m>\funcdef{T_C}{\bbR^3}{\bbR^3}</m> satisfies
		<md><mrow>
			T_C(\uvec{e}_1) \amp = \begin{abmatrix}{r} 2 \\ -3 \\ 5 \end{abmatrix} \text{,} \amp
			T_C(\uvec{e}_2) \amp = \begin{abmatrix}{r} -7 \\ 11 \\ 13 \end{abmatrix} \text{,} \amp
			T_C(\uvec{e}_3) \amp = \begin{abmatrix}{r} 17 \\ 19 \\ -23 \end{abmatrix} \text{.}
		</mrow></md>
		Do you have enough information to determine matrix <m>C</m>?
	</p></task>
</activity>

<activity xml:id="activity-lintrans-basic-axioms">
	<prelude><p>
		Due to the nice properties of matrix multiplication, 
        matrix transformations respect certain algebraic operations.
	</p></prelude>
	<introduction><p>
		Suppose <m>\funcdef{T_A}{\bbR^n}{\bbR^m}</m> is the matrix transformation associated to <m>m \times n</m> matrix <m>A</m>,
		so that <me> T_A(\uvec{x}) = A \uvec{x} </me>.
	</p></introduction>
	<task xml:id="activity-lintrans-basic-axioms-patterns">
		<introduction>
			<p>
			How does <m>T_A</m> interact with the vector operations of
			the <term>domain space</term> <m>\bbR^n</m>
			and the <term>codomain space</term> <m>\bbR^m</m>?
			</p><p>
			That is, how does <m>T_A</m> interact with
			</p>
		</introduction>
		<task><p> vector addition? </p></task>
		<task><p> scalar multiplication? </p></task>
		<task><p> linear combinations? </p></task>
		<task><p> negatives? </p></task>
		<task><p> the zero vector? </p></task>
	</task>
	<task xml:id="activity-lintrans-basic-axioms-axioms">
		<p>
		Which of the patterns from
		<xref ref="activity-lintrans-basic-axioms-patterns" text="type-local" />
		can be deduced from others of the patterns? 
        (Pause here and check with me!)
		</p><p>
		Based on this, we will make a more general definition.
	</p></task>
	<postlude><p>
		A function <m>\funcdef{T}{\bbR^n}{\bbR^m}</m> between <m>\bbR^n</m> and <m>\bbR^m</m>,  
        is often called a <term>transformation</term>. 
        A transformation <m>\funcdef{T}{\bbR^n}{\bbR^m}</m> 
        that satisfies the axioms we have identified in
		<xref ref="activity-lintrans-basic-axioms-axioms">Discovery</xref>
		will be called a
		<term>linear transformation</term>.
	</p></postlude>
</activity>

<activity xml:id="activity-lintrans-basic-exmps">
	<introduction><p>
		In each of the following,
		determine whether the provided vector space function is a linear transformation.
	</p></introduction>
	<task xml:id="activity-lintrans-basic-exmps-translate">
		<p> Translation by a fixed nonzero vector <m>\uvec{a}</m> in <m>\bbR^n</m>: </p>
		<p> <m>\funcdef{t_{\uvec{a}}}{\bbR^n}{\bbR^n}</m> by <m>t_{\uvec{a}}(\uvec{v}) = \uvec{v} + \uvec{a} </m>. </p>
	</task>
	<task xml:id="activity-lintrans-basic-exmps-scalar">
		<p> Multiplication by a fixed scalar <m>a</m> in <m>\bbR^n</m>: </p>
		<p> <m>\funcdef{m_a}{\bbR^n}{\bbR^n}</m> by <m>m_a(\uvec{v}) = a \uvec{v} </m>. </p>
	</task>
    <task>
        <p> Projection <m>\funcdef{\pi}{\bbR^3}{\bbR^2}</m> 
            of <m>\bbR^3</m> onto the <m>xy</m>-plane:</p>
        <p><m>\funcdef{\pi}{\bbR^3}{\bbR^2}</m> by <m>\pi\left(\threevec{x}{y}{z} \right) = \twovec{x}{y}</m></p>
    </task>
</activity>

<activity xml:id="activity-lintrans-basic-basis-images">
	<introduction><p>
		Suppose <m>H</m> is a subspace of <m>\bbR^n</m> with
		<me> H = \spn \{ \uvec{v}_1, \uvec{v}_2, \uvec{v}_3 \} </me>,
		and <m>\funcdef{T}{\bbR^n}{\bbR^2}</m> is a linear transformation such that
		<md><mrow>
			T(\uvec{v}_1) \amp = \twovec{1}{2} \text{,} \amp
			T(\uvec{v}_2) \amp = \twovec{3}{-5} \text{,} \amp
			T(\uvec{v}_3) \amp = \twovec{0}{4}
		</mrow></md>.
	</p></introduction>
	<task xml:id="activity-lintrans-basic-basis-images-lincomb-exmp"><p>
		Based on this information,
		can you determine <m>T(3 \uvec{v}_1 - \uvec{v}_2 + 5 \uvec{v}_3)</m>?
	</p></task>
	<task><p>
		Would you be able to answer
		<xref ref="activity-lintrans-basic-basis-images-lincomb-exmp" text="type-local" />
		for other vectors in <m>H</m>?
	</p></task>
	<task xml:id="activity-lintrans-basic-basis-images-pattern"><p>
		<em>Describe the pattern:</em>
		in order to be able to compute the output of a linear transformation for
        <em>every</em> vector in a subspace,
		the only output information required is
		<fillin characters="30" />.
	</p></task>
</activity>

<activity xml:id="activity-lintrans-basic-std-matrix">
	<introduction><p>
		Suppose <m>\funcdef{T}{\bbR^3}{\bbR^3}</m> is a linear transformation such that
		<md><mrow>
			T(\uvec{e}_1) \amp = \begin{abmatrix}{r} 2 \\ -3 \\ 5 \end{abmatrix} \text{,} \amp
			T(\uvec{e}_2) \amp = \begin{abmatrix}{r} -7 \\ 11 \\ 13 \end{abmatrix} \text{,} \amp
			T(\uvec{e}_3) \amp = \begin{abmatrix}{r} 17 \\ 19 \\ -23 \end{abmatrix} \text{.}
		</mrow></md>
	</p></introduction>
	<task xml:id="activity-lintrans-basic-std-matrix-compare">
		<statement><p>
			Do you know any other linear transformation <m>\bbR^3 \to \bbR^3</m> that has the same outputs for the standard basis vectors as inputs?
		</p></statement>
		<hint><p>
			Look back at <xref ref="activity-lintrans-basic-matrix-against-std-vecs">Discovery</xref>.
		</p></hint>
	</task>
	<task><p>
		Based on <xref ref="activity-lintrans-basic-std-matrix-compare" text="type-local" />,
		and in light of <xref ref="activity-lintrans-basic-basis-images-pattern">Discovery</xref>,
		what can you say about <m>T</m>?
	</p></task>
	<task><p>
		<em>Describe the pattern:</em>
		every linear transformation <m>\bbR^n \to \bbR^m</m> is effectively
		<fillin characters="30" />.
	</p></task>
</activity>

<activity xml:id="activity-lintrans-basic-linfunc-Rn-is-dot-prod">
	<task>
		<p>
		Suppose <m>\funcdef{T}{\bbR^n}{\bbR^1}</m> is a linear transformation.
		What size of matrix would represent this linear transformation?
		</p><p>
		What word would we normally use to describe a matrix of those dimensions,
		instead of <q>matrix</q>?
		</p>
	</task>
	<task><p>
		<em>Describe the pattern:</em>
		every linear transformation <m>\bbR^n \to \bbR^1</m> corresponds to a
		<fillin characters="30" />.
	</p></task>
</activity>

</worksheet>